---
import ProjectPageLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectPageLayout.astro';
import SpecTable from '../../components/SpecTable.astro';
import MetricCallout from '../../components/MetricCallout.astro';

const specRows = [
  { label: 'Injector Orifices',   value: '69'                    },
  { label: 'N₂O Error (Initial)', value: '35%'                   },
  { label: 'N₂O Error (Final)',   value: '10.3%'                 },
  { label: 'Model Type',          value: 'Two-phase, real-fluid' },
  { label: 'Thermodynamic Lib',   value: 'CoolProp'              },
  { label: 'Language',            value: 'Python 3'              },
];
---
<ProjectPageLayout
  title="Two-Phase N2O Flow Model"
  description="Python/CoolProp two-phase model reducing N₂O mass-flow error from 35% to 10.3%."
  ghostText="INJECTOR"
  category="UCLA Rocket Project"
  tagline="Two-phase N₂O model — 35% → 10.3% mass-flow error"
>
  <section class="spec-block">
    <SpecTable rows={specRows} title="Specifications" />
    <div class="todo-block">
      <span class="todo-label">TODO — Orifice pattern diagram</span>
      <p>Annotated diagram of the 69-orifice pattern: oxidizer/fuel port arrangement, orifice diameter, spacing.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="metrics-row" aria-label="Key metrics">
    <MetricCallout value="69"   unit="orifices" description="Flight injector orifices"  />
    <MetricCallout value="10.3" unit="%"         description="Final N₂O mass-flow error" />
    <MetricCallout value="3.4"  unit="×"         description="Error reduction factor"    />
  </section>
  <section class="the-work">
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Problem</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">The heritage single-phase incompressible injector model predicted N₂O mass flow with ~35% error against cold-flow data. N₂O near its saturation point exhibits real-fluid behavior — compressibility, phase transitions, non-ideal density — that single-phase models cannot capture, leading to systematic over-prediction and incorrect mixture-ratio assumptions.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Approach</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">Replaced the single-phase model with a Python/CoolProp two-phase real-fluid model. CoolProp provides thermodynamic properties (density, enthalpy, entropy) for N₂O as a function of temperature and pressure, enabling the model to account for phase transitions and compressibility. The corrected model was then used to size the 69-orifice flight injector against target mixture-ratio and mass-flow requirements.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Analysis</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <div class="todo-block">
          <span class="todo-label">TODO — Predicted vs. actual plot</span>
          <p>Chart comparing single-phase model, two-phase model, and static-fire measured mass flow. Interactive version lives on the Propulsion Data Analysis page.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="todo-block">
          <span class="todo-label">TODO — Model derivation writeup</span>
          <p>Governing equations, CoolProp integration method, and validation methodology. Rendered Markdown or PDF summary.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Result</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">Reduced N₂O mass-flow prediction error from 35% to 10.3% against static-fire data — a 3.4× improvement. Used the corrected model to size the 69-orifice flight injector. The two-phase model is now the team's standard tool for injector sizing and mixture-ratio verification.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Reflection</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">The model assumes steady-state flow and does not capture transient startup dynamics or combustion-driven oscillations. Future work includes transient startup modeling and discharge-coefficient sensitivity analysis across the orifice geometry space.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectPageLayout>
